/* From Redux_Handwritten_Easy_Notes.pdf (34 + 35) and Redux_2026_New_Trends_Handwritten_Easy_Notes.pdf (20).
   m = the memory line from the notes. */
(function () {
  const R = (lv, q, k, a, m, c) => ({ lv, q, k, a, m, c });
  HUB.add({
    id: 'reduxnotes', title: 'Redux: my notes (34 + 35 + 2026 trends)', short: 'Redux notes',
    blurb: 'Master flow: UI → dispatch(action) → middleware → reducer → store → UI re-render. Store = DATA, Action = WHAT, Reducer = DECIDE, Middleware = SIDE EFFECT, Selector = READ, RTK Query = SERVER DATA.',
    items: [
// ---------- Most important (34) ----------
R('Most important', `1. What is Redux?`,
  `One big toy cupboard for the whole app, so toys don't have to be passed hand to hand.`,
  `A JavaScript library for <b>predictable global state</b>: shared data lives in one central store, changes happen only through dispatched actions and pure reducers. Reduces prop drilling and makes every change traceable and debuggable (DevTools, time travel). Written today with Redux Toolkit.`,
  `Redux = ONE GLOBAL STORE.`),
R('Most important', `2. What are the core principles of Redux?`,
  `One cupboard, you can only ask for changes with notes, and a fair rule book decides.`,
  `<b>Single source of truth</b>: global state in one store. <b>State is read-only</b>: change it only by dispatching actions. <b>Changes with pure functions</b>: reducers compute the next state from (state, action) without mutation or side effects.`,
  `ONE → ACTION → PURE REDUCER.`),
R('Most important', `3. Explain the Redux data flow.`,
  `Press a button → send a note → the rule book makes a new cupboard → the screen updates.`,
  `The UI dispatches an action → middleware may run side effects → reducers calculate the next state → the store saves it and notifies subscribers → components reading changed data (via <code>useSelector</code>) re-render. Data flows in one direction.`,
  `A → R → S → UI.`),
R('Most important', `4. Redux store vs React state vs Context?`,
  `React state is your own pocket; Context is a loudspeaker; Redux is the house cupboard with a librarian.`,
  `<b>React state</b>: local to a component. <b>Context</b>: transports values down the tree (dependency injection), with all consumers re-rendering on change. <b>Redux</b>: global store independent of the tree, with structured updates (actions/reducers), selector-based subscriptions, middleware and DevTools.`,
  `State = local | Context = pass | Redux = manage.`),
R('Most important', `5. What are actions and action creators?`,
  `An action is a note saying what happened; an action creator is a note-writing machine.`,
  `An action is a plain object with a <code>type</code> (and usually a <code>payload</code>) describing an event. An action creator is a function returning that object, avoiding typos and repeated code. <code>createSlice</code> generates both.`,
  `Action = WHAT HAPPENED.`,
  `const itemAdded = item => ({ type: 'cart/itemAdded', payload: item })
dispatch(itemAdded({ id: 7 }))`),
R('Most important', `6. What are reducers, and what does Immer do?`,
  `The reducer is the rule book that decides the new cupboard; Immer lets you write the rules simply without breaking anything.`,
  `A reducer takes current state + action and returns the next state, immutably. RTK uses <b>Immer</b>: you write "mutating" code on a draft and Immer produces a new immutable state with structural sharing.`,
  `Reducer = DECIDES the new state.`,
  `reducers: { toggled(state, action) { const t = state.find(x => x.id === action.payload); t.done = !t.done } }   // safe with Immer`),
R('Most important', `7. What is middleware in Redux?`,
  `A checkpoint between sending a note and the librarian reading it.`,
  `Middleware wraps <code>dispatch</code> and sits between dispatch and the reducer: it can inspect, change, delay or block actions and run side effects (API calls, logging, analytics). Examples: thunk (default in RTK), saga, listener middleware, custom loggers.`,
  `Middleware = ACTION GATE.`),
R('Most important', `8. How do you connect Redux to React?`,
  `Put the cupboard at the top of the house, then bricks read with one sticker and write with another.`,
  `Wrap the app in <code>&lt;Provider store={store}&gt;</code>. Read with <code>useSelector</code>, send actions with <code>useDispatch</code> (typed versions in TS).`,
  `Provider → READ = selector → CHANGE = dispatch.`,
  `const count = useSelector(state => state.cart.items.length)
const dispatch = useDispatch()
dispatch(itemAdded(toy))`),
R('Most important', `9. What are selectors and createSelector?`,
  `A selector is a little question to the cupboard; createSelector remembers the answer until the toys change.`,
  `Selectors read or derive data from state. <code>createSelector</code> (Reselect) memoizes derived results: it recomputes only when input selectors return new references, keeping outputs stable and preventing re-renders.`,
  `Selector = READ; createSelector = READ + CACHE.`,
  `const selectTotal = createSelector([s => s.cart.items], items => items.reduce((t, i) => t + i.price * i.qty, 0))`),
R('Most important', `10. What is state normalization?`,
  `Instead of a messy pile, each toy gets a numbered shelf spot, and a list remembers the order.`,
  `Store collections flat: <code>entities</code> (objects by id) + <code>ids</code> (order). No duplicated nested data, O(1) lookups, targeted updates of one item. <code>createEntityAdapter</code> does it for you.`,
  `entities = DATA, ids = ORDER.`,
  `{ ids: ['u1', 'u2'], entities: { u1: { id: 'u1', name: 'Asha' }, u2: { id: 'u2', name: 'Ravi' } } }`),
R('Most important', `11. What are Redux DevTools?`,
  `A replay camera for the cupboard: see every note and rewind.`,
  `A browser extension/development tool recording every action and state change: action history, state diffs, time-travel debugging, skipping actions, import/export of history, trace of where actions were dispatched. Enabled automatically by <code>configureStore</code> in development.`,
  `DevTools = SEE + GO BACK.`),
R('Most important', `12. Immutability: spread vs Immer?`,
  `Spread: you copy every box by hand. Immer: a helper copies only what changed for you.`,
  `Without Immer you must spread every level you change (<code>{...state, user: {...state.user, address: {...}}}</code>), which is error-prone for deep data. Immer lets you write direct assignments on a draft and safely produces the immutable result.`,
  `Spread = manual; Immer = automatic.`),
R('Most important', `13. What are common Redux pitfalls?`,
  `Don't scribble on the cupboard directly, and only put plain toys inside.`,
  `Mutating state outside Immer; storing non-serializable values (Promises, functions, class instances, DOM nodes, Dates as objects) which break DevTools, persistence and time travel; duplicating derived data; putting server cache or every form keystroke in Redux; selectors returning new objects each time.`,
  `Redux likes PLAIN JSON DATA.`),
R('Most important', `14. When to use Redux vs local state vs Context?`,
  `Small and private: your pocket. Rarely-changing house rules: loudspeaker. Big busy shared data: the cupboard.`,
  `useState/useReducer for temporary local UI state; Context for low-frequency shared settings (theme, language, auth user); Redux for large, frequently updated, complex global data shared by unrelated parts of the app.`,
  `Local → Context → Redux as scope/complexity grows.`),
R('Most important', `15. What is the difference between Redux and Redux Toolkit?`,
  `Redux is the cupboard you build by hand; RTK is a ready-made cupboard kit.`,
  `Classic Redux needs manual store setup, action types, action creators, switch reducers and immutable updates. RTK provides <code>configureStore</code>, <code>createSlice</code>, <code>createAsyncThunk</code>, entity adapters, RTK Query and good defaults, cutting boilerplate drastically.`,
  `RTK = Redux made EASY.`),
R('Most important', `16. Why is Redux Toolkit recommended?`,
  `The official, easier, safer way, made by the same team.`,
  `It's the Redux team's official approach: less boilerplate, sensible defaults (thunk, DevTools), Immer for safe updates, built-in immutability and serializability checks in dev, TypeScript-friendly APIs, and RTK Query for server data.`,
  `RTK = OFFICIAL + LESS CODE.`),
R('Most important', `17. What is createSlice?`,
  `One page with the drawer's starting toys, its rules, and automatic note machines.`,
  `<code>createSlice({ name, initialState, reducers, extraReducers })</code> creates a reducer and auto-generates action creators/types. <code>extraReducers</code> lets the slice respond to other actions (other slices, thunk lifecycles).`,
  `Slice = STATE + REDUCERS + ACTIONS.`),
R('Most important', `18. What is configureStore?`,
  `A one-step cupboard builder with good settings already on.`,
  `Replaces <code>createStore</code> boilerplate: combines slice reducers, adds thunk and dev checks (immutability, serializability), enables DevTools, and accepts middleware and enhancer callbacks.`,
  `configureStore = STORE SETUP.`,
  `export const store = configureStore({ reducer: { cart: cartReducer, [api.reducerPath]: api.reducer }, middleware: g => g().concat(api.middleware) })`),
R('Most important', `19. What is createAsyncThunk?`,
  `A shop-trip helper that automatically says "started", "done" or "failed".`,
  `It wraps an async function and dispatches <code>pending</code>, <code>fulfilled</code> and <code>rejected</code> actions you handle in <code>extraReducers</code> for loading, data and error state. Supports <code>rejectWithValue</code>, <code>condition</code> and abort signals.`,
  `P → F → R = Pending → Fulfilled → Rejected.`),
R('Most important', `20. What is RTK Query?`,
  `A built-in helper with a notebook for server data: it fetches, remembers and refreshes.`,
  `RTK's data fetching and caching layer. Define an API with endpoints: <b>queries</b> read data, <b>mutations</b> change it; auto-generated hooks (<code>useGetToysQuery</code>); cache keyed by arguments; <b>tags</b> link mutations to cached queries for invalidation and refetching.`,
  `Query = GET; Mutation = CHANGE; Tags = REFRESH.`,
  `const api = createApi({
  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api' }),
  tagTypes: ['Toy'],
  endpoints: b => ({
    getToys: b.query({ query: () => 'toys', providesTags: ['Toy'] }),
    addToy: b.mutation({ query: body => ({ url: 'toys', method: 'POST', body }), invalidatesTags: ['Toy'] }),
  }),
})
export const { useGetToysQuery, useAddToyMutation } = api`),
R('Most important', `21. What is a feature-sliced folder structure?`,
  `Each room of the house keeps all its own things together.`,
  `Group by business feature (<code>features/auth</code>, <code>features/cart</code>: slice, selectors, API endpoints, components, tests) instead of technical folders (all reducers together). Shared store setup, API base and UI live in <code>app/</code> and <code>components/</code>.`,
  `ORGANIZE BY FEATURE.`),
R('Most important', `22. How do RTK Query tags and invalidation work?`,
  `Each notebook page gets a label; when the shop changes something, cross out pages with that label so they're fetched again.`,
  `Queries declare <code>providesTags</code> (e.g. <code>[{ type: 'Post', id }]</code>, plus a <code>'LIST'</code> tag); mutations declare <code>invalidatesTags</code>. After a mutation succeeds, matching cached queries are marked stale and refetched if subscribed.`,
  `Mutation → invalidate tag → refetch.`),
R('Most important', `23. RTK Query vs React Query/SWR?`,
  `RTK Query lives inside the Redux house; React Query and SWR are separate helpers that work anywhere.`,
  `RTK Query integrates with the Redux store, DevTools, middleware and slices (cache in Redux state, endpoints can affect slices). TanStack Query and SWR are independent of Redux with their own cache. Choose RTK Query when the app already relies on substantial RTK architecture; TanStack Query for non-Redux apps.`,
  `RTKQ = WITH REDUX; Query/SWR = INDEPENDENT.`),
R('Most important', `24. How do you persist Redux state?`,
  `Write down the cupboard before bed and read it back in the morning.`,
  `<code>redux-persist</code> saves selected slices to storage and rehydrates them on start; <code>PersistGate</code> delays UI until hydration finishes. Persist only what's needed (cart, preferences), version for migrations, never persist secrets or tokens in localStorage.`,
  `Persist = SAVE; Hydrate = LOAD.`),
R('Most important', `25. How do you test Redux?`,
  `Check the rule book alone, pretend to be the shop for trips, and play with the real screen.`,
  `Unit-test pure reducers and selectors (state + action → expected state). Test async logic with mocked network (MSW). Test connected UI with React Testing Library inside a real Provider with a fresh, preloaded store per test.`,
  `Reducer → Unit; API → MSW; UI → RTL.`),
R('Most important', `26. Should form state go in Redux?`,
  `Each letter you type stays in your pocket; only the finished form goes to the cupboard.`,
  `Usually no: keep in-progress input local (useState) or in React Hook Form; dispatch the final data on submit. Exceptions: multi-step wizards spanning routes, autosave drafts, or forms other parts of the app must observe.`,
  `FORM INPUT = LOCAL; APP DATA = REDUX.`),
R('Most important', `27. What are typed hooks in Redux?`,
  `Stickers that already know the cupboard's labels, so you can't ask for the wrong drawer.`,
  `<code>useAppSelector</code> and <code>useAppDispatch</code> bound to <code>RootState</code> and <code>AppDispatch</code>, giving autocomplete and compile-time safety (including thunks).`,
  `Typed hooks = SAFER Redux.`,
  `export const useAppDispatch = useDispatch.withTypes<AppDispatch>()
export const useAppSelector = useSelector.withTypes<RootState>()`),
R('Most important', `28. How do you avoid unnecessary selector re-renders?`,
  `If the answer is the same, hand back the same answer, not a fresh copy.`,
  `useSelector re-renders when the returned reference changes. Return primitives or existing references; avoid building new arrays/objects in inline selectors; use <code>createSelector</code> for derived data; select the smallest slice needed; use <code>shallowEqual</code> when returning small objects.`,
  `SAME INPUT → SAME REFERENCE.`),
R('Most important', `29. Redux + RTK Query vs Zustand/Jotai/Context?`,
  `Pick the size of cupboard that fits your house.`,
  `Redux + RTK Query for large, complex systems needing structure, middleware and DevTools; Zustand for minimal global stores with little boilerplate; Jotai for many independent atomic pieces of state; Context for low-frequency settings. Decide by complexity, team size and update patterns.`,
  `Choose by COMPLEXITY + UPDATE PATTERN.`),
R('Most important', `30. How do you manage authentication state in Redux?`,
  `A drawer that knows who's logged in, whether they're allowed in, and their short-life wristband.`,
  `A dedicated auth slice: <code>user</code>, <code>isAuthenticated</code>, <code>status</code>, short-lived access token (in memory, not persisted). Long-lived refresh token in an HttpOnly cookie, refreshed via thunk or an RTK Query <code>baseQuery</code> wrapper on 401. On logout, dispatch one action that every slice resets on, and reset the API cache.`,
  `AUTH = USER + STATUS + TOKEN FLOW.`),
R('Most important', `31. How do you handle deep nested updates?`,
  `Let the helper (Immer) copy the deep boxes for you.`,
  `With RTK + Immer, assign directly (<code>state.users[id].address.city = 'Hyd'</code>) or use push/splice on drafts. Without RTK, spread every nesting level. Better still, normalize so updates are shallow.`,
  `Deep state → LET IMMER DO IT.`),
R('Most important', `32. How do you handle theme and i18n with Redux?`,
  `House settings like colour and language live in a small settings drawer.`,
  `A global settings slice with primitive values (<code>theme</code>, <code>language</code>), persisted if desired. Selectors feed theme classes/CSS variables and an i18n library such as i18next (call <code>i18n.changeLanguage</code> in a listener).`,
  `theme + language = GLOBAL SETTINGS.`),
R('Most important', `33. When should you avoid Redux?`,
  `Don't build a giant cupboard for a tiny room.`,
  `Lightweight apps, static sites, simple forms, state used by one component, or apps whose main need is server caching (TanStack Query/SWR covers it). Adding Redux there adds complexity without benefit.`,
  `SIMPLE APP → DON'T ADD COMPLEXITY.`),
R('Most important', `34. What does serializability mean in Redux?`,
  `Only put things in the cupboard that can be written on paper.`,
  `State and actions should be plain serializable data (objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, null) so DevTools, persistence, SSR hydration and time travel work. Keep Promises, functions, sockets and DOM objects outside the store (in middleware, refs or module scope). RTK warns in dev.`,
  `Store = JSON-FRIENDLY DATA.`),
// ---------- Better to know (35) ----------
R('Better to know', `B1. What are lazy-loaded reducers and combineSlices?`,
  `Bring a room's drawer only when someone walks into that room.`,
  `RTK 2's <code>combineSlices</code> builds a root reducer that can inject slices later with <code>rootReducer.inject(slice)</code> when a route/feature loads, keeping the initial bundle small. Injected slices get typed selectors via <code>slice.selectors</code> and <code>withLazyLoadedSlices</code>.`,
  `combineSlices = LOAD REDUCER LATER.`,
  `export const rootReducer = combineSlices(authSlice, api)
// in the lazily loaded feature module:
const injected = rootReducer.inject(reportsSlice)`),
R('Better to know', `B2. Listener middleware vs custom middleware?`,
  `The listener is a ready-made watcher; custom middleware is building your own checkpoint from scratch.`,
  `Listener middleware (<code>createListenerMiddleware</code>) reacts to actions or state changes with async effects and built-in helpers: <code>delay</code>, <code>condition</code>, <code>take</code>, cancellation (<code>cancelActiveListeners</code>) for debounce/throttle. Custom middleware uses the low-level <code>store =&gt; next =&gt; action</code> signature; reserve it for cross-cutting concerns like logging or crash reporting.`,
  `Listener = EASY REACTIVE EFFECTS.`),
R('Better to know', `B3. How do optimistic updates work in RTK Query?`,
  `Put the new toy on the shelf now, keep a photo, and undo if the shop says no.`,
  `In a mutation's <code>onQueryStarted</code>, patch the cache with <code>updateQueryData</code> (returns a patch with <code>undo</code>), await <code>queryFulfilled</code>, and call <code>patch.undo()</code> on failure.`,
  `UPDATE FIRST → SERVER → UNDO IF FAIL.`,
  `updatePost: b.mutation({
  query: ({ id, ...patch }) => ({ url: 'posts/' + id, method: 'PATCH', body: patch }),
  async onQueryStarted({ id, ...patch }, { dispatch, queryFulfilled }) {
    const result = dispatch(api.util.updateQueryData('getPost', id, draft => { Object.assign(draft, patch) }))
    try { await queryFulfilled } catch { result.undo() }
  },
})`),
R('Better to know', `B4. How do you normalize users + posts?`,
  `Users on one shelf, posts on another, and each post only writes down its owner's number.`,
  `Separate collections <code>users.entities/ids</code> and <code>posts.entities/ids</code>; a post stores <code>userId</code>, not the whole user. Join in memoized selectors.`,
  `FLAT DATA + IDs.`),
R('Better to know', `B5. What is createEntityAdapter?`,
  `A ready-made shelf system with buttons for add, update, remove and find.`,
  `Generates normalized state (<code>{ ids, entities }</code>), CRUD reducers (<code>addOne</code>, <code>upsertMany</code>, <code>updateOne</code>, <code>removeOne</code>, <code>setAll</code>) and selectors (<code>selectAll</code>, <code>selectById</code>, <code>selectIds</code>), with optional sorting.`,
  `Adapter = CRUD + SELECTORS.`),
R('Better to know', `B6. What are the RTK 2.0 interview points?`,
  `The newer kit adds lazy drawers, better labels and stricter rules.`,
  `<code>combineSlices</code> and slice injection; slice <code>selectors</code>; <code>.withTypes()</code> helpers (createSelector, createAsyncThunk, hooks); <code>createStore</code> marked deprecated; object syntax for <code>extraReducers</code>/<code>createReducer</code> removed (builder callback only); action types must be strings; ESM-first packaging; Reselect 5 and Immer 10.`,
  `RTK 2 = LAZY SLICES + TYPES.`),
R('Better to know', `B7. How do slices communicate with each other?`,
  `One announcement, and every drawer that cares tidies itself.`,
  `Model events, not setters: one action (e.g. <code>auth/logout</code>) handled by many slices in <code>extraReducers</code>. Avoid slices importing each other's internals (circular deps); use shared action creators (<code>createAction</code>) or listeners for coordinated flows.`,
  `ONE ACTION → MANY SLICES.`,
  `extraReducers: b => { b.addCase(loggedOut, () => initialState) }`),
R('Better to know', `B8. How do you structure Redux for 200+ screens?`,
  `A huge house: every wing has its own drawers, and they only appear when you visit.`,
  `Feature modules with colocated slices, selectors and endpoints; lazy-inject slices with <code>combineSlices().inject</code>; split the RTK Query API with <code>injectEndpoints</code>; typed hooks; strict ownership boundaries and lint rules on imports.`,
  `BIG APP = FEATURES + LAZY LOAD.`),
R('Better to know', `B9. How do you separate UI state from server state in a SaaS app?`,
  `Things you're doing right now on screen vs things the shop owns.`,
  `Server state (profiles, billing, organisations) lives in RTK Query's cache. Transient UI state (sidebar open, modal, unsaved text) stays local or in small UI slices. Don't copy server data into slices.`,
  `UI STATE ≠ SERVER STATE.`),
R('Better to know', `B10. How would you model an e-commerce cart with promotions?`,
  `Keep the raw list (which toy, how many, which coupon); work out totals when you need them.`,
  `Store quantities keyed by <code>productId</code> and applied coupon codes. Never store subtotal/discount/tax; compute them with memoized selectors from current product prices and rules so they're always consistent.`,
  `STORE RAW; SELECT DERIVED.`),
R('Better to know', `B11. How do you build a multi-step wizard with autosave?`,
  `Remember which page you're on and everything typed so far; save a moment after you stop typing.`,
  `A wizard slice holds <code>currentStep</code> and accumulated <code>formData</code>. A listener watches update actions, debounces (cancel previous + delay), then saves via a thunk or RTK Query mutation.`,
  `TYPE → WAIT → AUTOSAVE.`),
R('Better to know', `B12. How do you handle WebSocket real-time updates in Redux?`,
  `A walkie-talkie listener turns every message into a normal note for the cupboard.`,
  `A listener (or RTK Query <code>onCacheEntryAdded</code> for streaming updates) opens the socket on login, parses messages, dispatches normal actions or patches the cache, and closes on logout. Keep the socket object outside the store.`,
  `SOCKET → ACTION → STORE.`),
R('Better to know', `B13. How do you build offline-first Redux?`,
  `When the road is closed, keep a queue of letters; send them when it reopens.`,
  `Persist relevant state; queue outgoing mutations while offline (with ids and timestamps); listen to online events and replay the queue; resolve conflicts (server wins / merge); show sync status.`,
  `OFFLINE = QUEUE; ONLINE = SYNC.`),
R('Better to know', `B14. How do you use Redux in micro-frontends?`,
  `Each mini-app keeps its own cupboard and they talk by sending messages.`,
  `Prefer isolated stores per micro-frontend, communicating via an event bus or custom DOM events. If one store must be shared, inject namespaced slices dynamically and agree on contracts.`,
  `MICRO APPS = ISOLATE.`),
R('Better to know', `B15. How do you migrate legacy Redux to RTK + RTK Query?`,
  `Swap old parts for kit parts one at a time.`,
  `Incrementally: <code>createStore</code> → <code>configureStore</code> (existing reducers keep working); convert modules to <code>createSlice</code>; replace manual spreads with Immer; replace hand-written API thunks and loading flags with RTK Query endpoints; swap <code>connect</code> for hooks as you touch components.`,
  `CONFIGURE → SLICE → QUERY.`),
R('Better to know', `B16. How do you model state for a collaborative app?`,
  `The shared document, who else is here, and a walkie-talkie for live changes.`,
  `Separate document data (normalized entities, versions, pending local operations) from presence (online users, cursors, selections). Apply remote patches from WebSocket listeners; reconcile with local pending ops (OT/CRDT libraries for text).`,
  `DATA + PRESENCE + SOCKET.`),
R('Better to know', `B17. The same data appears on 5 screens. What do you do?`,
  `One notebook page shared by five readers instead of five trips to the shop.`,
  `Use one RTK Query endpoint: hooks with the same arguments share one cache entry and one request; tags keep it fresh after mutations. No duplicated fetch logic or copies in slices.`,
  `ONE CACHE → MANY SCREENS.`),
R('Better to know', `B18. How do you optimise Redux performance?`,
  `Small careful answers, tidy shelves, and drawers that load only when needed.`,
  `Memoized selectors and small subscriptions, normalized data for targeted updates, code-split/lazy-injected reducers, RTK Query <code>selectFromResult</code>, batching, avoiding large non-serializable payloads, and profiling with React DevTools.`,
  `MEMO + NORMALIZE + LAZY.`),
R('Better to know', `B19. How do you handle feature flags / A-B testing?`,
  `A switchboard that says which new toys are turned on.`,
  `Load flags at startup (or via RTK Query) into a flags slice; components select the specific flag and render variants; keep defaults for safety and track exposure events.`,
  `FLAG → SELECT → SHOW.`),
R('Better to know', `B20. How do you design a dashboard with many widgets and real-time metrics?`,
  `Each widget has its own small drawer; live numbers arrive by walkie-talkie.`,
  `Split state by widget/domain, RTK Query per data source with polling or streaming updates, normalized entities where shared, memoized selectors per widget, and avoid one giant object that re-renders everything.`,
  `MANY WIDGETS = SMALL FEATURES.`),
R('Better to know', `B21. How do you build a notification center with queuing?`,
  `Notes arrive, wait in line, get shown, then get marked as read.`,
  `Normalized notifications plus queue/status (unread, displayed, dismissed). Listeners receive events and enqueue; a display listener shows one at a time with delays; mark-read actions update status without blocking the UI.`,
  `RECEIVE → QUEUE → DISPLAY → READ.`),
R('Better to know', `B22. How do you handle an infinite-scroll feed?`,
  `Keep each post once on the shelf and a bookmark of where you stopped.`,
  `Normalized items plus page cursors; append new ids; dedupe. With RTK Query 2.6+ use infinite query endpoints (<code>build.infiniteQuery</code>) or <code>merge</code>/<code>serializeQueryArgs</code> to accumulate pages.`,
  `ITEMS + CURSOR.`),
R('Better to know', `B23. Error boundaries + Redux logging: how do they relate?`,
  `A safety net for broken drawings, and a diary for what happened to the cupboard.`,
  `Error boundaries catch render errors and show fallback UI. Redux middleware/DevTools log actions and state for tracing state bugs; send both to monitoring (Sentry's Redux integration attaches recent actions to errors).`,
  `UI ERRORS ≠ STATE LOGGING.`),
R('Better to know', `B24. How would you structure state for a video streaming platform?`,
  `The player's controls, the "up next" list, and suggestions from the shop.`,
  `Player state (current video, position, playing, volume) in a focused slice (high-frequency position often kept local/refs); queue slice; recommendations and catalog via RTK Query caching.`,
  `PLAYER + QUEUE + RECOMMEND.`),
R('Better to know', `B25. How do you use Redux with SSR/Next.js?`,
  `The kitchen prepares the cupboard, then hands it to the house so they match.`,
  `Create a new store per request (never a module singleton on the server); preload data on the server; serialize state and hydrate the client store; in the Next.js App Router create the store in a client provider (useRef) and prefer Server Components/RTK Query for server data.`,
  `SERVER → HYDRATE → CLIENT.`),
R('Better to know', `B26. How do you prevent race conditions in async thunks?`,
  `If you asked twice, only the newest answer counts.`,
  `Track the latest <code>requestId</code> (from <code>action.meta.requestId</code>) and ignore stale fulfilled actions; abort previous requests via the thunk's returned <code>abort()</code> and <code>signal</code>; use <code>condition</code> to skip duplicate fetches; RTK Query handles this for you.`,
  `LATEST REQUEST WINS.`),
R('Better to know', `B27. How do you design a large admin/ERP store?`,
  `Departments each get their own drawers, loaded on demand, with tidy shelves for big tables.`,
  `Domain slices (inventory, billing, CRM) injected lazily; normalized tables via entity adapters; RTK Query with split endpoints and tags; server-side pagination/filtering; typed hooks and selectors per domain.`,
  `DOMAINS + LAZY + NORMALIZE.`),
R('Better to know', `B28. How do you rate-limit or debounce actions?`,
  `Wait until the kid stops typing, then send just one letter.`,
  `Listener middleware: on each matching action call <code>listenerApi.cancelActiveListeners()</code>, then <code>await listenerApi.delay(ms)</code>, then do the effect, so only the last call within the window runs.`,
  `TYPE FAST → WAIT → ONE CALL.`,
  `listener.startListening({
  actionCreator: searchChanged,
  effect: async (action, api) => {
    api.cancelActiveListeners()
    await api.delay(300)
    api.dispatch(searchApi.endpoints.search.initiate(action.payload))
  },
})`),
R('Better to know', `B29. Redux + design systems / Storybook?`,
  `Pretty bricks don't need the cupboard; connected bricks get a pretend cupboard in the showroom.`,
  `Keep presentational components Redux-free (props only). For connected components, add a Storybook decorator that wraps stories in a <code>Provider</code> with a mock/preloaded store; mock RTK Query with MSW.`,
  `UI PURE; CONNECTED WRAPPER.`),
R('Better to know', `B30. What is a testing strategy for a large Redux codebase?`,
  `Many quick small checks, some pretend-shop checks, a few whole-screen checks.`,
  `Testing pyramid: unit tests for reducers/selectors; async flows with MSW-mocked network; integration tests with RTL and a real store; a few E2E tests (Playwright). Avoid mocking useSelector/useDispatch.`,
  `UNIT → API → INTEGRATION.`),
R('Better to know', `B31. How do you maintain Redux at huge scale?`,
  `Each team owns its wing, with labels and lazy-loading.`,
  `Feature-sliced architecture, lazy slice injection, typed hooks, split RTK Query endpoints, colocated reducers/selectors/thunks/UI per domain, lint boundaries, codegen for APIs, and clear ownership to reduce cross-team conflicts.`,
  `MODULAR + TYPED + LAZY.`),
R('Better to know', `B32. How do you handle multi-tenant Redux?`,
  `When you switch to another family's house, clear the old family's things first.`,
  `Keep the active tenant at the root; scope cached data by tenant id (include it in query args/tags); on tenant switch, reset tenant-scoped slices and <code>api.util.resetApiState()</code>, cancel pending requests, then load the new tenant's data.`,
  `TENANT ID = DATA BOUNDARY.`),
R('Better to know', `B33. Critical optimistic UI with rollback: what must you add?`,
  `Do it now, try it, undo if it fails, and tell the user it was undone.`,
  `Apply the predicted change, run the request, undo on failure and show a localized error explaining the reversal; for critical operations (payments, bookings) prefer pessimistic updates.`,
  `DO NOW → TRY → UNDO.`),
R('Better to know', `B34. How do you handle a multi-route form wizard?`,
  `Pages change, but the notebook with your answers stays.`,
  `Central wizard slice holds all data and validation progress; each route uses local form handling (React Hook Form) and commits to the slice on step completion; a debounced listener persists to sessionStorage.`,
  `ROUTES CHANGE; DATA STAYS.`),
R('Better to know', `B35. Normalized vs denormalized state performance?`,
  `A tidy shelf lets you change one toy without disturbing the rest.`,
  `Deep denormalized trees change many references on each update and cause broad re-renders. Normalized entities/ids give targeted updates, stable references and O(1) lookups, at the cost of upfront flattening and joins in selectors.`,
  `NORMALIZED = TARGETED UPDATES.`),
// ---------- 2026 trends (20) ----------
R('2026 trend', `T1. What are RTK Query infinite queries?`,
  `Pages of toys kept together, with "load more" adding the next page.`,
  `<code>build.infiniteQuery</code> (RTK 2.6+) stores pages together with <code>getNextPageParam</code>/<code>getPreviousPageParam</code>; the hook returns <code>data.pages</code>, <code>fetchNextPage</code> and <code>hasNextPage</code>. Ideal for "Load more" and endless scroll.`,
  `Infinite = pages kept together.`),
R('2026 trend', `T2. What is prefetching in RTK Query?`,
  `Open the cookie jar before the kid asks.`,
  `<code>usePrefetch('getPost')</code> returns a function to call on hover/focus, or dispatch <code>api.util.prefetch(endpoint, arg, { ifOlderThan })</code>, so the next screen renders from cache instantly.`,
  `Pre-fetch = fetch before click.`),
R('2026 trend', `T3. What are polling and refetching options?`,
  `Ask the shop again every few seconds, or when you come back to the page.`,
  `<code>pollingInterval</code>, <code>skipPollingIfUnfocused</code>, <code>refetchOnMountOrArgChange</code>, <code>refetchOnFocus</code>, <code>refetchOnReconnect</code> (the last two need <code>setupListeners(store.dispatch)</code>), plus manual <code>refetch()</code>.`,
  `Polling = ask the server again.`),
R('2026 trend', `T4. What are skip and skipToken?`,
  `No question to ask yet? Don't go to the shop.`,
  `Pass <code>{ skip: !id }</code> or, better for TypeScript, <code>skipToken</code> as the argument so the hook doesn't run until required input exists.`,
  `No input → no request.`,
  `const { data } = useGetUserQuery(userId ?? skipToken)`),
R('2026 trend', `T5. What is selectFromResult?`,
  `Take only the one toy you need from the box, so you're not disturbed when other toys move.`,
  `An option on query hooks that picks part of the result; the component re-renders only when that part changes.`,
  `Select small → rerender less.`,
  `const { post } = useGetPostsQuery(undefined, {
  selectFromResult: ({ data }) => ({ post: data?.find(p => p.id === id) }),
})`),
R('2026 trend', `T6. How does RTK Query code splitting work?`,
  `Each room adds its own pages to the shared notebook.`,
  `Define one empty base <code>createApi</code>, then each feature calls <code>api.injectEndpoints({ endpoints })</code> in its own module, loaded lazily with that feature. <code>enhanceEndpoints</code> adds tags or options to generated endpoints.`,
  `Big app → split endpoints.`),
R('2026 trend', `T7. What is combineSlices in Redux Toolkit 2.x?`,
  `A drawer system where new drawers can be slotted in later.`,
  `Combines slices (and RTK Query APIs) into a root reducer that supports later injection with <code>.inject()</code> and lazy-typed selectors, enabling code-split Redux.`,
  `combine + inject = lazy Redux.`),
R('2026 trend', `T8. What is reducer injection?`,
  `The feature arrives, and its drawer arrives with it.`,
  `A lazily loaded feature module injects its slice into the root reducer when imported, so initial bundles don't include every feature's reducer and state.`,
  `Feature arrives → reducer arrives.`),
R('2026 trend', `T9. What can listener middleware do (advanced)?`,
  `A watcher that waits, cancels, and reacts to notes or changes in the cupboard.`,
  `<code>startListening</code> with <code>actionCreator</code>, <code>matcher</code> or <code>predicate</code>; effects can <code>take</code> future actions, <code>condition</code> on state, <code>delay</code>, <code>fork</code> child tasks, cancel previous instances (debounce/takeLatest), and be added/removed dynamically.`,
  `Action happens → listener reacts.`),
R('2026 trend', `T10. What is withTypes() in Redux Toolkit?`,
  `Write the labels once and reuse them everywhere.`,
  `Pre-typed helpers: <code>useSelector.withTypes&lt;RootState&gt;()</code>, <code>useDispatch.withTypes&lt;AppDispatch&gt;()</code>, <code>createAsyncThunk.withTypes&lt;{ state; dispatch; rejectValue }&gt;()</code>, <code>createSelector.withTypes&lt;RootState&gt;()</code>, <code>listenerMiddleware.startListening.withTypes</code>.`,
  `Type once → reuse.`),
R('2026 trend', `T11. How do RTK Query and SSR/Next.js work together?`,
  `The kitchen fetches, packs the notebook, and the house unpacks it.`,
  `Fetch on the server (per-request store, <code>api.endpoints.x.initiate</code> + <code>Promise.all(api.util.getRunningQueriesThunk())</code>), serialize state, rehydrate on the client (<code>extractRehydrationInfo</code>). In Next.js App Router, often fetch in Server Components and use RTK Query for client-side interactivity.`,
  `Server fetch → hydrate → client.`),
R('2026 trend', `T12. How do you handle errors, retry and cancellation?`,
  `Every shop trip has three endings: still walking, success, or failure, and you can cancel a trip.`,
  `Model loading/success/error explicitly; use <code>retry()</code> around <code>baseQuery</code> for transient failures (with maxRetries and bail-out on 4xx); abort stale thunks via signal; ignore outdated responses; show error UI with a retry action.`,
  `Request = loading + success + error.`),
R('2026 trend', `T13. What is an optimistic update with rollback?`,
  `Show the change first, take it back if the shop says no.`,
  `Patch the cache or state immediately, confirm with the server, undo the patch (or invalidate and refetch) on failure, and tell the user.`,
  `UI first, rollback if fail.`),
R('2026 trend', `T14. What can createEntityAdapter do (advanced)?`,
  `A shelf system with built-in buttons, sorting and finders.`,
  `<code>sortComparer</code>, <code>selectId</code> for custom keys, <code>upsertMany</code> for merging API pages, <code>getSelectors(selectState)</code> for global selectors, and using adapters inside RTK Query <code>transformResponse</code> to normalize cached data.`,
  `Many items → normalize.`),
R('2026 trend', `T15. What is RTK Query OpenAPI code generation?`,
  `The shop's menu is used to print the order forms automatically.`,
  `<code>@rtk-query/codegen-openapi</code> reads an OpenAPI schema and generates typed endpoints and hooks, keeping client types in sync with the backend; customise with <code>enhanceEndpoints</code>.`,
  `API schema → generated code.`),
R('2026 trend', `T16. What is auto-batching in Redux Toolkit?`,
  `Many small changes, one "the cupboard changed" announcement.`,
  `<code>autoBatchEnhancer</code> (included by configureStore in RTK 2) delays subscriber notifications for actions tagged as low priority (RTK Query uses this), reducing repeated renders; React 18 also batches state updates automatically.`,
  `Many updates → fewer notifications.`),
R('2026 trend', `T17. What should you know about RTK 2.x migration?`,
  `Know the old way so you can read it; build the new way.`,
  `Move to configureStore, slices, builder-callback extraReducers, RTK Query, listener middleware, combineSlices; note ESM packaging, string-only action types, deprecated <code>createStore</code>, and React-Redux 9 requiring React 18+.`,
  `Legacy = understand; RTK = build.`),
R('2026 trend', `T18. What is the modern side-effects rule?`,
  `Shop data → the notebook helper; "when this happens, do that" → the watcher; special errands → thunks.`,
  `Server data: RTK Query. Action/state-driven workflows (autosave, websockets, analytics, debounced effects): listener middleware. One-off imperative async flows: thunks. Sagas only for legacy or very complex orchestration.`,
  `Data → RTK Query; workflows → Listener.`),
R('2026 trend', `T19. What is the interview architecture pattern for modern Redux?`,
  `Screen → stickers → drawers and notebook → cupboard.`,
  `UI components → typed hooks (<code>useAppSelector</code>, generated RTK Query hooks) → slices/selectors for client state + RTK Query for server state → RTK-configured store (combineSlices, listener middleware, API middleware).`,
  `UI → hooks → slice/API → store.`),
R('2026 trend', `T20. What should you not over-focus on in Redux interviews?`,
  `Don't spend all your time learning the old hand-built cupboard.`,
  `Legacy handwritten action-type constants, switch reducers, manual immutable updates, <code>connect</code> and <code>mapStateToProps</code>: know them for maintenance, but prepare mainly modern RTK patterns. 1-minute answer: "Modern Redux is Redux Toolkit: slices and configureStore for client state, RTK Query for server data, listener middleware for workflows, combineSlices for large apps, TypeScript for safety."`,
  `Legacy = understand; RTK = build.`),
    ],
  });
})();
